Taylor H: Ran into a nasty onboarding footgun: default sampleapp + paginated embedding + streaming generation -> client-side backpressure and unstable memory. Repro included.

Taylor H: Steps: start sampleapp (defaults), spin up 15 clients that iterate pages where each page does an embed call and then streams a generation for the page. After ~30s we see a flurry of 429s, then the process piles up pending promises and memory climbs.

Samir N: Any stack traces or logs? Looks like a classic inflight-request explosion.

Taylor H: log bits:
```
2026-03-25T09:12:01Z WARN request 429 retry-after=2 path=/v1/generate
2026-03-25T09:12:04Z INFO worker: requeued retry attempt=1 queue_len=48
2026-03-25T09:12:08Z ERROR uncaught: RangeError: Invalid typed array length
    at KVStore.alloc (kvstore.js:91:14)
```

Harpreet K: Two simultaneous issues usually: (A) retries dropping into a separate path that doesn't respect the concurrency pool, and (B) streaming responses or large KV cache allocations per sequence accumulating when clients keep starting new sequences.

Zoe P: Is auth refresh involved? We've seen stampedes amplify memory usage.

Taylor H: We see occasional refresh attempts but not a full stampede here. The bigger issue seems to be retries + streaming buffers. The sampleapp uses a naive per-request semaphore set to 128 by default and recursive retry on 429. Pseudocode from sampleapp:
```js
const sem = new Semaphore(128);
async function processPage(cursor){
  await sem.acquire();
  try {
    const emb = await client.embed(pageText);
    const stream = client.streamGenerate({prompt});
    for await (const chunk of stream) { buffer.push(chunk); }
    return buffer;
  } catch(e) {
    if (isRetryable(e)) return await processPage(cursor); // recursion -> bad
    throw e;
  } finally { sem.release(); }
}
```

Samir N: That recursion is a re-entrancy bug: retrying without re-acquiring the slot (or if you do re-acquire inside recursion, you may exceed limits). Also streaming buffers held in memory until completion will multiply. We should: (1) cap buffer size per sequence, (2) turn recursion into an explicit retry loop that acquires a slot for each try, and (3) for streaming, back-pressure the upstream by pausing reading when buffer hits a cap.

Harpreet K: Agreed. I recommend default sem=16 for onboarding sampleapp and a separate flag `--high-throughput` for advanced users. Also add a per-stream soft cap (e.g., 256KB) and a policy to drop/trim old chunks when exceeded plus emit a warning to logs.

Zoe P: SDK parity: JS has single-flight refresh and streaming helpers, but our Rust SDK has onboarding examples that still refresh per request and have no streaming backpressure helpers. We should align the Rust SDK to provide a stream API that exposes pause/resume and a MaxStreamBuffer option. That's unique from other parity asks and worth adding.

Samir N: Good call. Also for retries we should respect Retry-After and server-suggested pacing headers, and queue retries behind the same concurrency control. Example retry-loop pattern to adopt in sampleapp and SDKs:
```js
async function retryWithSlot(fn, maxAttempts=5) {
  let attempts = 0;
  while (++attempts <= maxAttempts) {
    await sem.acquire();
    try { return await fn(); }
    catch (e) {
      if (!isRetryable(e) || attempts === maxAttempts) throw e;
    } finally { sem.release(); }
    const wait = Math.min(1000 * 2 ** attempts, 5000) + jitter();
    await sleep(wait);
  }
}
```

Taylor H: I wired a quick local patch: sem default 128 -> 16, streaming buffer cap 256KB, and replaced recursion with the retryWithSlot pattern. Re-ran with same workload: 429s still occur briefly but no memory spike and workers recover.

Harpreet K: Nice. Action items:
- Zoe: PR to Rust SDK to add single-flight token refresh + streaming backpressure API + MaxStreamBuffer config (default 256KB).
- Samir: update sampleapp with retryWithSlot, sem default 16, and stream buffer cap; add CLI flag `--high-throughput` to restore old defaults when explicitly requested.
- Harpreet: add telemetry events: sdk.client.retry.queue_length, sdk.client.stream.buffer_size, sdk.client.refresh.inflight_count.
- Taylor: attach repro harness and flame graph to ticket ONB-6002.

onboard-bot: Created ticket ONB-6002: "onboard: streaming backpressure, retry queue, Rust SDK parity" assigned to Zoe P, Samir N, Harpreet K.

Zoe P: I'll scope the Rust changes to two small pieces: (1) example client that uses a shared refresh future (single-flight), (2) stream reader that exposes pause()/resume() and a MaxStreamBuffer option in Config. I'll also add an integration test that simulates a slow consumer and ensures no unlimited buffer growth.

Samir N: I'll add a CLI health check that warns when users set concurrency > 64 or unset stream caps. Also update quickstart docs with a "How to safely run high-throughput on Dedicated" subsection.

Harpreet K: We'll want to add a short onboarding blurb: "If you see many 429s during quickstart, enable --high-throughput only after reading dedicated deployment docs; otherwise run with defaults."

Taylor H: Thanks! I'll attach my small repro harness and the before/after flame graphs to ONB-6002. Re-running looks stable.

Zoe P: :rocket: planning PRs today.
